# ADR Format and Lifecycle

Store ADRs in `docs/adr/` and use numeric, kebab-case file names:

- `0001-short-title.md`
- `0002-next-decision.md`

## ADR Structure

Use this section order:

1. Title
2. Status
3. Date
4. Context
5. Decision
6. Alternatives Considered
7. Consequences
8. References

## Lifecycle Rules

- Keep one architecture decision per ADR.
- `accepted` ADRs stay editable until the decision is implemented or released,
  even if the status has not been updated yet. Note each change in the ADR.
- Set the status to `implemented` once the decision is implemented or released.
  From then on it can only be superseded.
- When an implemented ADR's direction changes, create a new ADR and mark the old
  one superseded.
- ADRs accepted before this rule (0068 and lower, except 0053 and 0067) can only
  be superseded, whatever their status says. Their status line may still be
  updated.
- Link old and new ADRs for traceability.

## Traceability Rules

When implementation follows an ADR, reference the ADR in:

- commit message or PR description
- implementation notes
- related technical documentation
